WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.540
Welcome everyone, good afternoon,

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:03.200
my name is Elissa Strome,

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.640
I'm the executive director

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.200
of the Pan-Canadian AI strategy at CIFAR

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.190
and also the Associate
Vice President research.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.250
And, it's my pleasure
to welcome you all today

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.380
to CIFAR's webinar

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.670
on science is the exit strategy.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:19.480
This is a new series of
webinars that we're offering

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.430
to share with our community

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:24.450
research that's going on by
CIFAR researchers fellows,

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.810
Canada CIFAR AI chairs in Canada

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.750
and around the world to share

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.140
how science and science
based decision making

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.380
can be used and is being
used by governments

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.622
to help us think about how
we can reopen the economy,

00:00:36.622 --> 00:00:38.950
(mumbles) in the wake of COVID-19.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:40.840
If you're not familiar with us,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.360
CIFAR is a Canadian based
global charitable organization,

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.400
and we convene extraordinary minds

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.340
to address the most important questions,

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.470
facing society and humanity.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.270
Like many others across the globe,

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.380
CIFAR has pivoted quickly
to take action on COVID-19

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.550
and we've done a lot of
work in the last nine weeks

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.270
to mobilize researchers across
Canada and around the world

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.080
to help them and support them,

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.883
advance research on COVID.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.520
What I'd like to do today
to kick off is introduce you

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.480
to two researchers that
we work with at CIFAR.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.040
Marzyeh Ghassemi is a
Canada CIFAR AI chair

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.170
at the Vector Institute

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.210
and Assistant Professor at
the University of Toronto

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.930
where she also holds a
Canada Research Chair.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.060
Her work focuses on applying machine,

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.160
learning to understand and improve health.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.530
Joseph Paul Cohen, is
a Postdoctoral Fellow

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:33.250
with Yoshua Bengio at Mila in Montreal,

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.790
and at the University of Montreal.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:37.730
His work examines the
applications of AI in medicine

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.530
with respect to computer vision,

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:42.200
genomics, clinical data, et cetera.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.740
So Marzyeh and Joseph
have a new collaboration

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.190
that has emerged as a result of COVID-19

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.400
and then thinking,

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.360
collaboratively about how
they can work together

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.020
to address some of these issues.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.250
Their project will use AI

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.140
to develop approaches

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.260
to understanding COVID-19

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.500
based on chest X-rays

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.580
and so we're gonna explore

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.460
and other types of medical imaging

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.030
so we're gonna explore their
project a little bit more.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.420
Before we begin, though,

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.840
just a few sort of housekeeping issues,

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.660
the session today will last
for approximately 30 minutes

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.300
and I'm gonna sort of
kick off the conversation,

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.870
but then we'll open up
the floor for questions.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.300
So we would love for the audience

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.490
to send in your questions,

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.763
please use the Q&amp;A function,

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:25.640
and not the chat function

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.440
cause we'll be sort of monitoring,

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.420
your questions that come in through Q&amp;A

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.470
and we'll make sure that
we save plenty of time

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.750
for those questions.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.360
And lastly, I just
wanna let everybody know

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.430
that the session is being recorded.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.080
So if you wanna go back
and look at it later

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.280
or send it along to your friends,

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.460
we'll have it up on the CIFAR website,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.010
early next week.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:44.800
Okay, so let's begin.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.470
Marzyeh and Joseph, thank
you for joining us today

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:48.560
to share your work

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.080
and to be part of this conversation.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.530
Let's start, Joseph

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.230
by talking a little bit
about the work that you did

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.820
last year you built a
platform called Chester

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:02.020
sort of, ironically named.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.110
An AI, chest X-ray
radiology assistant tool

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.230
that your team develops,

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.570
and now you're pivoting

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:09.630
and thinking about how to
apply some of that work,

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:12.170
and the knowledge and
tools that you created

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:13.900
towards COVID-19.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.090
And so, the first step in
your project with Marzyeh

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:17.970
is to build a public dataset

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.960
of chest X-ray, CT images

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.210
maybe other medical images as well.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.976
And so, how are you guys
going about doing that?

00:03:24.976 --> 00:03:25.810
What are the steps you're taking?

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.800
And, what are some of the
challenges that you're facing?

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.100
- Yeah so, initially we
had this Chester tool

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.010
and it already predicted pneumonia,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.970
given datas that existed before

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:39.400
from like the NIH,

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:40.943
MIT, Stanford.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:44.340
And, we wanted to see does this work

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.930
on the COVID pneumonia cases.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:48.770
Is it able to offer an assistance there

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.720
and we looked around for
what data to try this on,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.360
we couldn't find any.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:54.630
And we contacted a bunch of groups

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.530
and it seems very difficult

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:58.700
to get data from hospital groups.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.260
They're all so very busy

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:03.110
and hospitals that aren't busy,

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:04.530
don't have any data yet,

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.050
or it didn't have any data at that time.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:08.140
So was really difficult

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.500
to get a handle on all this data,

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.370
and it seems like the,

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:16.090
infrastructure in the hospitals was not,

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:18.350
able to get us this data.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:19.670
There was no infrastructure in place

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:21.480
to allow researchers access to

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.410
data as it's happening, right?

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:26.320
So this was unfortunate

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.580
for us there.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.700
But we found that

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.060
journals journal publications,

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.233
did have these images in them.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:36.360
Researchers were publishing

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.520
like one or two patients at
a time in these publications,

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:40.470
and then publishing them,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.540
these high resolution

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.360
or CEP kind of high
resolution digital images

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:45.450
on the journal web pages

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.510
of like radiology, (mumbles).

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:50.790
So, in these online publications,

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.510
we can access a digital version

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.260
that's actually pretty high
resolution compared to what

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:57.550
these models were trained on

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.030
because we would resize them
to be pretty small images like,

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.350
224 by 224 resolution images.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:04.350
The images on these websites

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:05.840
were thousands but thousands, right?

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.120
So, we had like a
sevision resolution there.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.550
So we just started
aggregating these images

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:13.500
into a data set we could work with

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.850
by manually going to each web page,

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:17.880
downloading the image,

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.343
and then reading the
publication and finding,

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.040
the different kind of clinical outcomes,

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.110
and the different attributes
about these patients

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:28.360
that were already published there.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.660
And this was really
good for us because the,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.290
the ethics and consent,

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:33.470
were already taken care of

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.090
before this paper was published, right?

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.470
So that made it very
easy to work with as in,

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:39.990
in inside the ethical framework

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:41.490
of the University of Montreal,

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.260
cause it's this is this,
if it's already public

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:44.550
and it's already had all this consent on

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:45.720
we can use it,

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:46.940
so we can get going right out the gate

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:48.540
with all these images, right?

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.120
So this allowed us to start
building up this dataset,

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:52.953
which is now,

00:05:52.953 --> 00:05:53.786
we now have hundreds

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:55.880
of frontal chest X-rays

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:58.450
from different patients, right?

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:00.940
With multiple time points.
- Great

00:06:02.055 --> 00:06:03.640
and even around the world I guess too.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:04.473
- Yeah, all over the,

00:06:04.473 --> 00:06:07.020
which is a good way to
validate these models,

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.620
because we don't wanna
just have one hospital

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:09.680
that could have a,

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.080
an artifact that makes it

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.580
just work there but not other places,

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:15.000
we can really, test tools,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.300
this way which has been

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.919
often how this data set has now been used

00:06:19.919 --> 00:06:21.000
by a few groups

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.080
where they have their own
internal data set that they make.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.730
And then when they actually
go to validate their model,

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.080
but they do it on an internal data set

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:29.180
to really prove it'll work

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:30.730
cause they can have a balanced cohort

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:32.990
that's like nice and randomly sampled.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:35.240
But then, they can

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.680
publicly use the data that we put together

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:41.510
to demonstrate that their
model's working, right?

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:42.720
So this has been good

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:44.810
and beyond publications
we're also looking at

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.770
these repositories that exist.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:49.130
There's a EURORAD system

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:51.000
which has been phenomenal as a resource,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.460
because their licensing
is very clear for us to

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:54.563
use for research.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:58.300
So these different repositories
that have been put up

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:01.170
that are, they're kind of silos themselves

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:06.020
of this COVID chest X-ray cases, right?

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:07.760
So they're, not only do
they have chest X-rays,

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.540
they have CT slices.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:10.680
Some of them have CT's,

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.000
like full CT volumes.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.280
So this is this massive data

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:17.380
that's tucked in all
these different systems

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:18.330
all over the world.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:21.150
And in, where we've been

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.386
slowly putting it together
into this data set,

00:07:23.386 --> 00:07:24.320
and then from the small data set,

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:26.543
we can start training these models.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:31.090
- So it sounds like it's a
very labor intensive effort

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:33.440
just to even build the data set,

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.410
in order to be able to
build the models and then,

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:37.640
go on further and do research.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.570
- Yeah, it's very hard to
build automatic scrapers.

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:41.410
We've had some,

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:44.310
some automation in this.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.300
We just, we got some good results

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.460
on our EURORAD automatic scraper

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:51.140
so we can scrape a bunch of the,

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.850
detailed like the images,

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.000
we can get them

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.450
properly scraped with some basic metadata.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:00.240
But, clinical outcomes
really hard to scrape from,

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.550
from natural text

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:03.100
that radiologists are writing

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.920
because you don't know
if it says that they,

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:08.170
tested for COVID and it was negative,

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:11.460
or that they tested

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:12.820
for COVID and it was positive, right?

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:15.360
So that the way it's
worded can be just wild

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.810
so, the best thing is to
just have a human reader.

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:19.300
So we've been doing that.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:21.123
And we have dedicated students and,

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.827
like people looking at these things.

00:08:24.827 --> 00:08:27.840
And I've spent a lot of time,
staring at these reports and,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:28.700
learning a lot about,

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:30.090
I feel like, you really,

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:31.020
learn more about the disease

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:32.910
by reading a (mumbles) bunch of case notes

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:34.300
from doctors all over the world,

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:36.430
which scared me a lot, so,

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.240
just reading these case stories,

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.956
are very, sometimes sad.

00:08:41.956 --> 00:08:42.789
- It brings it home a little bit,

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:44.290
makes it a little more real, right?

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:46.103
Great.
- Oh.

00:08:46.103 --> 00:08:47.170
- Okay, so Marzyeh,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.250
I know that a key
objective of your project

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.720
is to develop some new
machine learning algorithms

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:54.880
in order to be able to
analyze these machine,

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.690
these medical images
that you're aggregating

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:58.890
across many many different sources.

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:00.620
And so, what like,

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:02.040
why and how is that challenging?

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.670
What were some of the
challenges that you face?

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:07.550
And, like, particularly
from the fact that these

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:10.050
images are being compiled
from so many different sources

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:11.990
in different modalities,

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:12.823
in different languages,

00:09:12.823 --> 00:09:14.450
maybe the case notes are
in different languages,

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:16.460
what are some of the
challenges that you have

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:18.230
in actually developing the AI tools

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:20.283
once you got the data set together?

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:23.560
- I think there are a couple of things

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.480
that make this very challenging.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:28.002
I think that the,

00:09:28.002 --> 00:09:28.835
the first thing

00:09:28.835 --> 00:09:30.910
to sort of keep in mind is.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:33.760
We know from work at NeurIPS,

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.910
one of the large machine
learning conferences

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:37.260
this past year,

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.420
in December of 2019

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.080
that medical image statistics

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.090
don't follow natural
image statistics, right?

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:46.070
So, one of the reasons

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:49.120
that convolutional neural
networks are so efficient

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.680
at doing object detection

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:53.580
or classification,

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:56.560
or any of the other many tasks that they,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.620
they work fantastically well on

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:01.350
is because there are these
natural image statistics,

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.930
and there are things

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.940
that you can assume in many cases,

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:07.430
about this modality, right?

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:09.210
So if two things are close in

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:11.000
pixel space, right?

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.870
They're either close in real life,

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:15.380
or there's a border there, right?

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:20.260
And those kinds of
assumptions don't work in,

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:21.330
imaging modalities,

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.560
in the same way, right?

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.710
Because it's not actually
just a natural image.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.360
And so, we know that

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.030
there are differences in how

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:31.730
you may want to fine-tune,

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:34.140
or take pre-trained deep neural networks

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:36.050
and apply them to medical imaging,

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:39.193
so that's its own
challenge in its own right.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:41.923
There's also a lot of,

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:47.710
uncertainty about differences and

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:49.340
severity of cases,

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:51.310
and also labels for cases, right?

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:53.890
So, one of the projects that,

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.860
one of the postdocs in
my group was looking at,

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:58.530
she's looking at these labels

00:10:58.530 --> 00:10:59.770
that are shared

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:01.710
and in some cases not shared

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:04.380
between large chest
X-ray imaging data sets

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:06.700
so these are data sets
that are put out by MIT

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:07.860
by Stanford,

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:09.520
by the NIH.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:11.480
And they have some overlap,

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:13.340
in some of the clinical labels

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:15.600
that are assigned to the images,

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:17.590
but it's not complete so,

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:20.920
some places will label for

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:22.440
a particular condition

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:23.273
a pneumothorax

00:11:23.273 --> 00:11:24.970
and others we won't.

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:26.780
And this also reflects differences

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:30.120
in what those actual care
centers tend to specialize in

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:33.510
and the populations that
they see in general.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.740
So those I think those are
very challenging because again,

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:38.330
when we develop models in,

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.804
the natural world for things like

00:11:40.804 --> 00:11:42.090
object detection.

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:43.890
We know object detection is important

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.280
because humans do it.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:47.050
It's a task that humans do.

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:49.520
And we know that a dog
is a dog is a dog, right?

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:50.840
We can argue about

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:52.130
what breed of dog

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:54.060
and how old the dog is,

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:55.450
but we all agree

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:57.190
that this is a dog, right?

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:00.760
And, you could take, an untrained human,

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:01.760
not a specialist,

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:04.450
not somebody who has gone to
medical school for a decade,

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:07.050
and every untrained human will agree

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:08.850
that this is a dog, right?

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.950
But you can have two expert radiologists

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:12.860
who have gone to school for decades,

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:14.470
and they will disagree

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:16.980
about the label that should be assigned

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:18.750
to this image.

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:20.930
So this is also incredibly challenging

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:23.680
for machine learning
models to operate under.

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:24.660
And then, finally,

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:27.000
one of the things that's
really challenging,

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:29.100
specifically in this setting,

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:32.670
is that this is a novel condition.

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.930
And that's something that we're trying to

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:36.110
wrap our heads around

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:38.030
in terms of how to do

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:39.900
what is called few shot,

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:42.250
meaning I see a few examples,

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.330
and then I'm quickly able to generalize

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:46.370
or zero shot.

00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:48.920
Meaning, I've not seen any examples

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:50.160
of this particular condition

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.700
but I'm able to detect
it as a new condition.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:56.060
And these are specific
regimes in machine learning,

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:57.120
but they're often

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:00.610
when you use these specific regimes

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:02.840
often it's in very controlled environments

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:06.960
where you promise that you'll
only show the algorithm

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.600
five classes during its training

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:10.833
and its learning phase.

00:13:12.528 --> 00:13:13.970
And then when you show it new things,

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:15.560
you'll show it only new things,

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:17.950
but none of you won't
evaluate it on the old things

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:20.240
that it might have forgotten, right?

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:21.870
And this is not a,

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:23.730
and so many of the evaluations

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:25.690
that are present in these sort of

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:29.350
larger learning scenarios don't apply here

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:31.030
because we don't want to

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:34.380
show a model many
examples of a pneumothorax

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:38.780
and of a hernia, all
these different things,

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:41.040
a fracture and then show it,

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.640
some COVID examples and
then have it forget.

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:45.120
Well what does a fracture look like?

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:46.430
I don't remember.

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:49.533
Additionally, we have these
multiple labels, right?

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:52.100
When you have one X-ray image

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:53.700
you could have a fracture,

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:55.100
and pneumonia,

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:56.040
that's possible, right?

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:56.970
And so, you have these

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:58.760
multiple labels that are toggled on,

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:00.670
but very rarely is

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:02.820
one object multiple objects.

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:03.920
It's not both a dog

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:05.640
and a cat, right?

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:07.600
You don't have cat dogs

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:08.433
that are coming around.

00:14:08.433 --> 00:14:10.920
And so, I think there are all of these

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:12.840
technical challenges,

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:15.470
where you can't just
take off the shelf models

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:19.280
or tools, algorithms
that have been developed

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:21.530
because they're often
developed in a setting

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:23.660
again for natural images mostly

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:25.870
where we don't have any
of these constraints

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:29.380
and so there's been a lot
of work between the students

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:30.940
and the postdoc that Joseph and I

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:32.430
are co-supervising

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:34.630
to try to understand how we can,

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:37.840
modify a lot of these meta learning,

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:40.340
few shot learning, zero
shot learning algorithm

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:42.800
so that they work in this setting.

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:44.400
- Yeah, I think what strikes me

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.815
from how you've described it is that,

00:14:46.815 --> 00:14:48.800
COVID-19 is an entirely new phenomenon

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:50.020
that we haven't seen before.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:52.070
