00:29:14.810 --> 00:29:16.450
How do you balance the need

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:17.900
for fast growing research

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:19.730
and pre-print publication,

00:29:19.730 --> 00:29:21.690
with the rigor and peer review

00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:23.010
during the pandemic, right?

00:29:23.010 --> 00:29:24.670
So, we wanna get our results out

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:27.090
we wanna share with the world.

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:29.220
But it's not the same thing

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:31.060
as a peer reviewed publication.

00:29:31.060 --> 00:29:32.723
So, how do you balance that guys?

00:29:34.970 --> 00:29:35.803
- So, I think.

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:38.420
Nothing should change,

00:29:38.420 --> 00:29:41.190
because why were we not being critical

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:42.530
about the papers in the past,

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:44.120
we just blindly trusted

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:46.260
the peer review.
- Right.

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:47.261
- Yeah.

00:29:47.261 --> 00:29:48.120
So I think,

00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:50.590
if a paper is hard to understand,

00:29:50.590 --> 00:29:52.560
and therefore, and for that reason,

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.040
you can't like believe that it's right

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:57.720
cause you don't understand the
entire experimental protocol,

00:29:57.720 --> 00:30:00.190
then don't trust the paper right like,

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:01.700
why should peer review

00:30:01.700 --> 00:30:02.677
allow someone else to tell you,

00:30:02.677 --> 00:30:04.120
you should trust that (mumbles)

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:06.530
just more work to filter
these papers yourselves,

00:30:06.530 --> 00:30:07.520
you should always be doing this

00:30:07.520 --> 00:30:09.405
with every paper that (mumbles).

00:30:09.405 --> 00:30:10.270
- And so how important is it right now

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:12.540
to get your results out

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:14.720
in the pre-prints world?

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:18.640
- I think it's becoming
increasingly apparent

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:23.640
that the, the style of
early release of results,

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:27.000
that is, I should say in different

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:29.120
academic communities
have very different ways

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:30.930
that they tend to push results out

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:33.100
and the machine learning
community for a while now

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:34.720
has had a culture

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:39.330
of publishing a pre-print
on a service like Archive.

00:30:39.330 --> 00:30:42.720
And then, as it is undergoing peer review.

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:45.740
And then, you may see for
example this is common,

00:30:45.740 --> 00:30:47.870
I just wanna emphasize
how common this is that,

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:50.080
a paper is posted in September

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:52.490
of 2018 on Archive.

00:30:52.490 --> 00:30:53.790
It is submitted somewhere

00:30:53.790 --> 00:30:54.950
and doesn't make it into

00:30:54.950 --> 00:30:57.120
that particular machine
learning conference, right?

00:30:57.120 --> 00:30:59.110
Unlike many of the journals where

00:30:59.110 --> 00:31:01.220
there's this, year long back and forth

00:31:01.220 --> 00:31:04.490
where well, edit did
this, cite this, add this,

00:31:04.490 --> 00:31:06.940
you have this one shot, that is,

00:31:06.940 --> 00:31:08.360
mildly stochastic, right?

00:31:08.360 --> 00:31:10.110
Depends on what reviewers you get.

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:11.450
You often don't have a rebuttal

00:31:11.450 --> 00:31:12.750
in some of these conferences

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:15.360
and so you might submit to NeurIPS

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:16.837
and it doesn't get into NeurIPS.

00:31:16.837 --> 00:31:18.490
And so, that's fine,

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:20.210
we say like re-roll, right?

00:31:20.210 --> 00:31:22.240
You submit essentially the same paper

00:31:22.240 --> 00:31:25.100
with some corrections or clarifications

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:27.320
as recommended by the reviewers

00:31:27.320 --> 00:31:28.610
to ICML

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:32.820
or to if it's more data
sensors focus to KDD to,

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:35.063
wherever you're submitting it to, right?

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:38.290
So I don't think that
culture should change.

00:31:38.290 --> 00:31:41.270
I'm happy that other publishing cultures

00:31:41.270 --> 00:31:43.340
are coming more in line with that.

00:31:43.340 --> 00:31:46.830
I think that that puts significantly more

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:50.350
a much higher expectation
of rigor on the PI.

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:52.530
So my students,

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:54.230
one of my previous students

00:31:54.230 --> 00:31:57.000
really wanted to Archive their work,

00:31:57.000 --> 00:31:58.460
because they felt some pressure

00:31:58.460 --> 00:32:01.620
and I told the student it's not ready.

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:03.210
And they said why it's not peer reviewed,

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:04.180
it's just Archive

00:32:04.180 --> 00:32:06.349
and I said no like this is.

00:32:06.349 --> 00:32:07.780
- (mumbles) Ready to go, yeah.

00:32:07.780 --> 00:32:08.900
- It has to be ready to go.

00:32:08.900 --> 00:32:11.270
This has to pass my sniff test

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:12.680
because it has our names on it

00:32:12.680 --> 00:32:13.730
and it means that

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:15.650
we're putting this out
there for consumption

00:32:15.650 --> 00:32:17.570
and you don't want to mislead people.

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:20.620
And so, I think that it
shifts the burden from a

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:22.400
well let's just throw
this out to peer review

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:24.310
we'll see if they spot a mistake,

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:25.230
to saying like,

00:32:25.230 --> 00:32:27.230
this is an academic product

